   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi Joseph,</div><div><br></div><div>The typical way that I&#39;ve set this up is to use NA placeholders for the specific factor input, though this isn&#39;t the only way. You could also specify the same model with the mirt.model() structure, like so:</div><div><br></div><div>library(mirt)<br>data(SAT12)<br>data &lt;- key2binary(SAT12,<br>                   key = c(1,4,5,2,3,1,2,1,3,1,2,4,2,1,5,3,4,4,1,4,3,3,4,1,3,5,1,3,1,5,4,5))<br><br># alternative model definition via ?mirt.model syntax<br>specific2 &lt;- &quot;S1 = 7,9,10,11,13,15,17,18,21,22,24,27,31<br>              S2 = 1,3,6,8,16,29,32<br>              S3 = 2,4,5,12,14,19,20,23,25,26,28,30&quot;<br>mod2 &lt;- bfactor(data, specific2)<br><br># omit lots of specific factor loadings for S2 (only general)<br>specific3 &lt;- &quot;S1 = 7,9,10,11,13,15,17,18,21,22,24,27,31<br>              S2 = 1,3,6,8<br>              S3 = 2,4,5,12,14,19,20,23,25,26,28,30&quot;<br>mod3 &lt;- bfactor(data, specific3)<br>coef(mod3, simplify=TRUE)<br></div><div><br></div><div>HTH.</div><div><br></div><div><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_signature\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Phil</div></div></div></div><br></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Fri, Aug 1, 2025 at 5:04 PM Joseph Miller &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">mllr.e...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
